+#### 3a. Cancellation, geometry and a uniform remainder — revision 3
+
+The algebraic cancellation already follows from the hypotheses of §3.
+The positive lower bound does not. The purpose of this clarification is
+to turn the proposed “positive leftover” into an explicit proof obligation,
+without treating it as a new physical mechanism or an established gap.
+
+For the same compact-link model, expand the kinetic term and integrate
+the cross terms by parts. On the stated smooth physical form core,
+
+\[
+\begin{aligned}
+q[f\psi_0]
+&:=\langle f\psi_0,(H-E_0)f\psi_0\rangle\\
+&=\kappa\int\psi_0^2\sum_{\ell,a}|X_{\ell a}f|^2\,dU\\
+&\quad+\int |f|^2\psi_0
+\left[-\kappa\sum_{\ell,a}X_{\ell a}^2\psi_0
++(V-E_0)\psi_0\right]dU.
+\end{aligned}
+\]
+
+The bracket is zero by the ground-state equation. The remainder is
+exactly the Dirichlet form \(\mathcal E_\nu\) already defined above.
+This cancels explicit potential and cross terms in an identity; it does
+not cancel the effect of the dynamics. The potential enters through
+\(\psi_0\), the measure \(\nu\), and the domain. This calculation is at
+the finite regulator, not a subtraction of uncontrolled infinities.
+
+Three meanings of geometry must remain distinct:
+
+| Geometry or descriptive choice | What can be simplified | What still needs control |
+|---|---|---|
+| Gauge coordinates | Remove redundant descriptions or work with invariant functions | The Gauss-law physical subspace, its measure, and the induced kinetic form |
+| Configuration/orbit-space geometry | Express its contribution through an inequality or comparison constant | The metric/gradient and vacuum weight; flat spacetime does not make this space flat |
+| Regulator geometry: lattice, volume and boundaries | Seek a limit insensitive to the chosen regulator | Prove that the theory and the lower bound survive the specified limits |
+
+S19 explores weighted curvature of the configuration space as one way
+to obtain gap estimates. S20 makes the conditional inputs especially
+explicit. Neither source establishes a general boundary beyond which
+geometry ceases to matter. A valid proof could use geometric information
+only to establish a constant; that is different from proving it irrelevant.
+
+Let \(r\) denote an admissible regulator, let \(P_{0,r}\) project onto its
+ground space, and let \(s_r>0\) be a specified reference energy in the
+same units as \(H_r\). A sufficient target is
+
+\[
+q_r[\psi]\geq\delta_*s_r\|(I-P_{0,r})\psi\|^2
+\quad\text{for every physical }\psi,
+\qquad\delta_*>0\text{ independent of }r.
+\]
+
+Here the statement is about quadratic forms on their full domains;
+an identity on a core must be extended using the justified closed form.
+The reference scale must approach a finite, nonzero value after conversion
+to the chosen physical units; defining it as the candidate gap would make
+the normalization circular.
+In the positive ground-state representation this becomes
+
+\[
+\mathcal E_{\nu_r}(f,f)\geq
+\delta_*s_r\operatorname{Var}_{\nu_r}(f)
+\quad\text{for every physical }f.
+\]
+
+Since \(\mathcal E\) includes the kinetic coefficient, the equivalent
+sufficient condition on a Poincaré upper bound is
+\(C_rs_r\leq\delta_*^{-1}\). This avoids confusing a bound in lattice
+units with a fixed physical scale. The density comparison in §3 is one
+possible source of a bound; it helps only if its constants have the
+required regulator dependence. Merely defining the best \(C_r\) by the
+observed gap supplies no independent estimate.
+
+The remainder must also be robust to every approximation used in the
+argument. For example, write a proposed decomposition on the physical
+form domain as \(q_r=D_r+R_r\). If independently established estimates give
+
+\[
+D_r[\psi]\geq d_*s_r\|(I-P_{0,r})\psi\|^2,
+\qquad
+|R_r[\psi]|\leq e_*s_r\|(I-P_{0,r})\psi\|^2,
+\qquad d_*>e_*\geq0,
+\]
+
+then \(\delta_*=d_*-e_*\) is a valid lower bound. This is an illustrative
+certificate format, not a decomposition proved here for Yang–Mills.
+Small errors on a few sampled states do not supply the displayed
+full-domain error estimate.
+
+The following failures are already distinguishable mathematically:
+
+- **Vacuum offset:** \(H\mapsto H+cI\) changes both \(H\) and \(E_0\);
+  their difference is unchanged. A scalar energy offset is not a gap.
+- **Pointwise positivity:** positive energy for every normalized vector
+  orthogonal to the vacuum may coexist with a sequence of such energies
+  tending to zero. One common positive lower bound is required.
+- **Boundary-generated gap:** for \(-\kappa\partial_x^2\) on a periodic
+  circle of length \(L\), the gap above the constant ground state is
+  \(4\pi^2\kappa/L^2\), which vanishes as \(L\to\infty\). This is a
+  scalar control, not a Yang–Mills calculation.
+- **Finite-basis success:** minimizing a Rayleigh quotient over a subset
+  of physical trial functions gives an upper bound on the full-domain
+  infimum. It cannot certify the lower bound above.
+
+Even a uniform regulated estimate must accompany a construction of the
+intended nontrivial continuum theory and a justified passage of the form
+bound to that limit. At nonzero theta or without a positive ground
+function the particular transform used here requires a different treatment.
+The next mathematical milestone is an independent estimate controlling
+this remainder in a specified model; the current pilot remains an
+instrument-calibration study.
